## 3️⃣  `consume_status` – safely consume a raw `TF_Status*` and delete it  

```cpp
// tf_helpers.hpp (or a dedicated utils header)

#pragma once
#include "tf_status.hpp"          // brings in tf::Status
#include <string_view>
#include <source_location>

namespace tf {

inline void consume_status(
    TF_Status* raw,
    std::string_view context = "",
    std::source_location loc = std::source_location::current())
{
    if (!raw) return;                 // nothing to do

    // Wrap the raw pointer in a temporary RAII object.
    // The destructor will call TF_DeleteStatus for us.
    Status st{raw};

    // Propagate the error (if any) with the richer diagnostics.
    st.throw_if_error(context, loc);
    // When the function exits, `st` is destroyed → TF_DeleteStatus(raw)
}
} // namespace tf
```

*Why this matters* – The original stub never deleted the `TF_Status*`. This version **always** frees the resource, even when an exception is thrown, because the destructor runs during stack unwinding.

